# bazel/bazelrc -- user-wide Bazel limits, deployed as ~/.bazelrc
# (Guix Home: home/common.scm; native: make setup-native-links).
#
# Why: a Bazel build in orb-guix (7 GiB RAM, 10 CPUs) used up all of the
# container's memory.  Bazel's defaults assume the whole machine is its own:
# the server JVM may grow to a quarter of RAM, and the scheduler runs one
# local action per CPU, each counted as only ~250 MB however much it really
# uses.  These lines leave room for everything else running alongside it
# (agents, editors, the Herdr server).
#
# A workspace's own .bazelrc is read after this file and can override any
# line.  Lift a limit for a single run on the command line, e.g.
#   bazel build --local_resources=cpu=HOST_CPUS //...
# These flags are valid for Bazel 7 and later (checked against 9.3.2).

# Server JVM: cap its heap, and shut it down after 15 idle minutes instead of
# the default 3 hours, so an idle server does not sit on gigabytes.
startup --host_jvm_args=-Xmx2g
startup --max_idle_secs=900

# Local action scheduling: half the CPUs and half the RAM.  Too many
# compile/link actions in parallel is what actually exhausts memory.
# HOST_RAM comes from /proc/meminfo, not from a cgroup limit: on a host with
# a cgroup memory cap, give an absolute value instead (memory=3072, in MB).
build --local_resources=cpu=HOST_CPUS*.5
build --local_resources=memory=HOST_RAM*.5
